WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.448
This is research from
the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:03.448 --> 00:00:06.462
Workbook's Research section,
and it's still under review.

4
00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:13.075
Federal HR 2.0 moves agencies' core
HR records onto one Oracle platform.

5
00:00:13.075 --> 00:00:16.666
If an agency builds HR-driven
identity on top of it,

6
00:00:16.666 --> 00:00:22.008
so a hire becomes an account and a
departure removes one, that data crosses

7
00:00:22.008 --> 00:00:27.087
Oracle's cloud, Microsoft's cloud, the
agency's own network, and maybe AWS.

8
00:00:27.087 --> 00:00:30.250
Every hop starts with a DNS lookup.

9
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.102
So we'll ask which names
must resolve where,

10
00:00:33.102 --> 00:00:36.850
what breaks, and when you need
private DNS or forwarding.

11
00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:42.131
Facts come from sources opened on October
twenty-second, 2026, a few Microsoft ones

12
00:00:42.131 --> 00:00:47.770
from search excerpts, and I'll flag inferences
and program design guidance as we go.

13
00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:53.433
Let's walk the chain end to end.

14
00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:59.744
It starts in Oracle Fusion Cloud HCM,
the HR system Oracle runs as a service.

15
00:00:59.744 --> 00:01:04.160
Changes leave Fusion as extracts
or change feeds, and middleware,

16
00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.249
running in Oracle's integration
cloud or in Azure, pulls them,

17
00:01:08.249 --> 00:01:13.645
turns them into a standard format called
SCIM, and posts them to Microsoft Graph.

18
00:01:13.645 --> 00:01:19.149
Entra then writes the user into Entra ID,
and for users still in Active Directory,

19
00:01:19.149 --> 00:01:22.651
a small on-premises agent
writes the change into AD.

20
00:01:22.651 --> 00:01:26.320
Lifecycle Workflows run the
joiner and leaver tasks.

21
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:32.507
Now notice the DNS pattern: every hop in
the top row crosses a vendor's public name,

22
00:01:32.507 --> 00:01:36.070
and only the agent-to-AD
hop needs internal DNS.

23
00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.100
That's the key idea, so let's look closer.

24
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:45.475
Here's the idea the rest
of the talk builds on.

25
00:01:45.475 --> 00:01:51.199
Almost every name in this path is public
and owned by a vendor: Oracle's Fusion

26
00:01:51.199 --> 00:01:53.653
host, Microsoft Graph and sign-in,

27
00:01:53.653 --> 00:01:57.287
the agent's relay names,
the Amazon Connect names.

28
00:01:57.287 --> 00:02:02.116
The agency's job is to resolve those
correctly from every resolver a component

29
00:02:02.116 --> 00:02:06.276
can reach, and never to host or shadow
them in a private zone of its own.

30
00:02:06.276 --> 00:02:12.041
Private DNS and forwarding are needed in
only four places: names the agency owns,

31
00:02:12.041 --> 00:02:17.546
like AD domains, cloud private endpoints
the middleware uses, cloud private

32
00:02:17.546 --> 00:02:23.487
zones on-premises systems must read, and
AWS interface endpoints with private DNS.

33
00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:26.526
Everything else is ordinary
public resolution.

34
00:02:26.526 --> 00:02:28.870
So where does each component look?

35
00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:36.048
This table maps each component
to what it must resolve,

36
00:02:36.048 --> 00:02:38.333
and the full version is in the backup.

37
00:02:38.333 --> 00:02:42.334
The HR middleware resolves Fusion,
Graph and sign-in publicly,

38
00:02:42.334 --> 00:02:46.648
but its evidence store and key
vault sit behind private endpoints,

39
00:02:46.648 --> 00:02:51.120
so it needs a linked private zone or
a resolver that forwards to Azure.

40
00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.258
Lifecycle Workflow extensions, as an
inference, can reach only public names.

41
00:02:56.258 --> 00:02:59.527
The provisioning agent needs
domain controller records

42
00:02:59.527 --> 00:03:02.320
internally and Microsoft's
relay names publicly.

43
00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.178
On-premises systems reach cloud
private names by forwarding

44
00:03:06.178 --> 00:03:09.658
from the Infoblox Grid to each
cloud's inbound endpoint.

45
00:03:09.658 --> 00:03:13.670
And VMware hosts need forward
and reverse records in the Grid.

46
00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:16.450
Next, a trap that fails quietly.

47
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.725
The government clouds use different names,
and getting this wrong fails quietly.

48
00:03:24.725 --> 00:03:29.175
A GCC tenant keeps using the
worldwide Graph and sign-in endpoints,

49
00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:33.382
while GCC High uses dot-us
addresses, and Microsoft says access

50
00:03:33.382 --> 00:03:38.075
tokens from one national cloud aren't
interchangeable with another's.

51
00:03:38.075 --> 00:03:40.793
The provisioning agent
has its own list of relay

52
00:03:40.793 --> 00:03:43.375
names that change in the
government cloud too.

53
00:03:43.375 --> 00:03:46.889
It needs no inbound ports,
and Microsoft's guidance,

54
00:03:46.889 --> 00:03:51.575
from search excerpts, is to avoid
inline TLS inspection on that path.

55
00:03:51.575 --> 00:03:55.556
The practical lesson is simple:
keep a per-cloud list of names,

56
00:03:55.556 --> 00:03:58.025
and test through the real egress path.

57
00:03:58.025 --> 00:04:00.940
Now to Oracle, and a common mistake.

58
00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:08.660
Oracle Fusion offers private
access from on-premises,

59
00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.953
and it sounds like a DNS feature.

60
00:04:10.953 --> 00:04:12.490
It isn't; it's routing.

61
00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:17.904
Traffic rides Oracle's private circuit or
a VPN into an Oracle network and on to

62
00:04:17.904 --> 00:04:23.402
Oracle's public address ranges, and neither
Oracle page says anything about DNS.

63
00:04:23.402 --> 00:04:26.859
As an inference, the Fusion
name keeps its public answer,

64
00:04:26.859 --> 00:04:28.964
so don't build a private zone for it.

65
00:04:28.964 --> 00:04:34.769
A second point: Oracle says its change
feeds can be periodically purged, so, as an

66
00:04:34.769 --> 00:04:40.752
inference, if the middleware loses DNS
longer than that window, HR events are lost.

67
00:04:40.752 --> 00:04:44.081
That makes middleware
DNS a correctness issue,

68
00:04:44.081 --> 00:04:47.752
not just uptime, so reconcile
against full extracts.

69
00:04:47.752 --> 00:04:50.802
Oracle Fusion is listed
at FedRAMP Moderate.

70
00:04:50.802 --> 00:04:53.230
Next, two more components.

71
00:04:56.980 --> 00:04:58.330
Two more components.

72
00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:02.560
Amazon Connect agents work in a
browser, and AWS says that traffic

73
00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:06.555
traverses the public network
and prefers a domain allowlist.

74
00:05:06.555 --> 00:05:09.601
If agents can't resolve
the media relay names,

75
00:05:09.601 --> 00:05:14.705
the softphone can't set up its media
channel, so don't over-restrict their DNS.

76
00:05:14.705 --> 00:05:18.859
In GovCloud, Connect is available
only in the US-West region,

77
00:05:18.859 --> 00:05:22.080
uses its own names, and
lacks several features.

78
00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.574
Private API access covers only
a subset of Connect, and,

79
00:05:26.574 --> 00:05:31.367
as an inference, helps API calls,
not the agent or voice path.

80
00:05:31.367 --> 00:05:35.458
On VMware, a Broadcom article
says changing vCenter's name

81
00:05:35.458 --> 00:05:39.293
or address is blocked if the
name doesn't fully resolve.

82
00:05:39.293 --> 00:05:41.417
So keep those records right in the Grid.

83
00:05:41.417 --> 00:05:43.480
Next, the subtle one.

84
00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:48.505
Here's the subtle one.

85
00:05:48.505 --> 00:05:50.902
Suppose a cloud network has both a private

86
00:05:50.902 --> 00:05:53.367
zone and a forwarding
rule for the same name.

87
00:05:53.367 --> 00:05:54.406
Which wins?

88
00:05:54.406 --> 00:05:56.155
The three clouds disagree.

89
00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:58.380
In AWS the rule wins.

90
00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:02.818
In Azure the private zone wins,
and the rule is silently ignored.

91
00:06:02.818 --> 00:06:08.142
In Oracle the private view wins, and
evaluation stops even on a negative answer.

92
00:06:08.142 --> 00:06:11.900
And a private zone that covers
a name but has no record for

93
00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:15.817
it says the name doesn't exist,
instead of asking anyone else.

94
00:06:15.817 --> 00:06:18.403
So, as an inference, the only design that

95
00:06:18.403 --> 00:06:21.717
behaves the same everywhere
is one owner per name.

96
00:06:21.717 --> 00:06:25.990
The Grid owns agency zones, each
cloud owns its own private zones,

97
00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:28.005
and everything else forwards.

98
00:06:28.005 --> 00:06:29.600
So how does it break?

99
00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:37.775
The brief lists sixteen ways this
breaks, and the slide picks seven.

100
00:06:37.775 --> 00:06:42.812
Shadowing is the classic: someone creates
a private zone for a vendor's domain,

101
00:06:42.812 --> 00:06:47.613
say oraclecloud dot com, and every name
not copied into it stops resolving.

102
00:06:47.613 --> 00:06:49.466
Silent fallback is sneakier:

103
00:06:49.466 --> 00:06:54.486
a private name resolves to the public
address because a rule is missing or slow,

104
00:06:54.486 --> 00:06:59.274
which is why Windows forwarders to
Azure need a timeout above four seconds.

105
00:06:59.274 --> 00:07:04.238
The Grid itself can fall back to the internet's
root servers if its forwarders fail.

106
00:07:04.238 --> 00:07:09.291
Then there are loops, certificate name
mismatches, the wrong cloud's endpoints,

107
00:07:09.291 --> 00:07:13.800
and capacity, so size resolver
endpoints before each onboarding wave.

108
00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.130
So how should forwarding be wired?

109
00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:24.818
With several clouds, every vendor documents
a hub, and none recommends a mesh.

110
00:07:24.818 --> 00:07:26.955
Here's some illustrative arithmetic.

111
00:07:26.955 --> 00:07:29.946
With five environments,
a full mesh needs up to

112
00:07:29.946 --> 00:07:33.330
twenty one-way rule sets,
while a hub needs about five.

113
00:07:33.330 --> 00:07:37.765
As an inference, every extra path is
one more place for the precedence,

114
00:07:37.765 --> 00:07:40.255
fallback and loop failures we just saw.

115
00:07:40.255 --> 00:07:44.600
The Infoblox Grid is a natural hub
for agency names, and Infoblox's

116
00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:49.830
reference design puts its virtual
appliance in a shared-services hub network.

117
00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:53.148
The earlier multi-cloud
research, as design guidance,

118
00:07:53.148 --> 00:07:57.521
recommends the Grid plus each cloud's
native resolver as the baseline,

119
00:07:57.521 --> 00:08:01.518
with customer-operated appliances
for FedRAMP High workloads.

120
00:08:01.518 --> 00:08:03.830
So what does the agency actually do?

121
00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:11.867
Here's what the agency must do,
condensed from a thirteen-step checklist,

122
00:08:11.867 --> 00:08:15.355
and it's program design guidance,
not a vendor requirement.

123
00:08:15.355 --> 00:08:20.921
Start with names and owners: inventory
every name in the HR path per cloud, never

124
00:08:20.921 --> 00:08:23.918
create a private zone
for a vendor's domain,

125
00:08:23.918 --> 00:08:26.830
and give each agency namespace one owner.

126
00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:31.735
Then forwarding: forward from the Grid
to inbound endpoints in two regions,

127
00:08:31.735 --> 00:08:35.453
raise Windows forwarder timeouts,
run a hub in each cloud,

128
00:08:35.453 --> 00:08:38.617
and size endpoints before
each onboarding wave.

129
00:08:38.617 --> 00:08:43.662
Then the components: run at least two
provisioning agents, treat middleware

130
00:08:43.662 --> 00:08:48.067
DNS as a data-integrity control,
and keep VMware records in the Grid.

131
00:08:48.067 --> 00:08:49.981
And test from every origin.

132
00:08:49.981 --> 00:08:54.460
Any public answer or missing name
for a private name is a defect.

133
00:08:58.210 --> 00:08:59.135
To close.

134
00:08:59.135 --> 00:09:01.871
The decisions are who owns each namespace,

135
00:09:01.871 --> 00:09:07.188
which middleware platform and so which
resolver path, the hub design in each cloud,

136
00:09:07.188 --> 00:09:10.549
and whether Oracle's private
access is needed at all,

137
00:09:10.549 --> 00:09:12.973
remembering that it's routing, not DNS.

138
00:09:12.973 --> 00:09:15.922
The next steps are the things
that couldn't be verified,

139
00:09:15.922 --> 00:09:19.789
such as which Oracle environment the
agency's tenant will use and what's

140
00:09:19.789 --> 00:09:23.460
available in the government clouds;
they're all listed in the backup.

141
00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:29.238
In the workbook, Book 03 covers HR-driven
identity and Book 08 covers DNS,

142
00:09:29.238 --> 00:09:35.898
DHCP and address management, and three
related research items sit beside this one.

143
00:09:35.898 --> 00:09:40.822
This is research under review, not an
endorsement by any federal agency.

144
00:09:40.822 --> 00:09:41.650
Thank you.
